WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.615
First, before any procurement:
no OMB memorandum, directive,

2
00:00:04.615 --> 00:00:08.901
executive order or NIST
publication names a DDI vendor,

3
00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:13.000
or requires a product category
called a DDI platform.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.812
They require capabilities.

5
00:00:14.812 --> 00:00:22.298
SP 800-53's SC-20, SC-21 and
SC-22 ask for signed answers,

6
00:00:22.298 --> 00:00:28.187
validating resolvers and fault-tolerant,
role-separated name service.

7
00:00:28.187 --> 00:00:34.576
OMB M-08-23 still requires
DNSSEC on applicable systems.

8
00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:39.879
M-22-09 requires encrypted DNS
wherever it's technically supported,

9
00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:43.362
to agency-designated resolvers
that log the queries.

10
00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:50.018
Executive Order 14144's section on encrypted
DNS survived the June 2025 amendments:

11
00:00:50.018 --> 00:00:56.008
agencies enable it within 180 days
wherever clients and servers support it,

12
00:00:56.008 --> 00:01:00.096
and CISA is directed to
publish contract language

13
00:01:00.096 --> 00:01:03.424
for any product that
acts as a DNS resolver.

14
00:01:03.424 --> 00:01:09.200
Civilian agencies must use CISA's
Protective DNS for all egress resolution.

15
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.788
And TIC 3.0 lists the
matching DNS capabilities.

16
00:01:13.788 --> 00:01:15.787
On logging, be precise.

17
00:01:15.787 --> 00:01:24.912
M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31 in May 2026,
and it doesn't mention DNS at all.

18
00:01:24.912 --> 00:01:28.949
It sets retention: six months
searchable, twelve retrievable.

19
00:01:28.949 --> 00:01:33.616
CISA's Logging Reference
Architecture then maps the old DNS,

20
00:01:33.616 --> 00:01:39.837
DHCP and passive DNS categories to network
activity, and calls them foundational.

21
00:01:39.837 --> 00:01:45.125
That's guidance mapped to a baseline,
not a standalone DNS mandate.

22
00:01:45.125 --> 00:01:53.035
For addresses, BOD 23-01 asks for discovery
across all agency IPv4 space every seven

23
00:01:53.035 --> 00:02:00.715
days, BOD 26-04 asks for quarterly attestation
of public IP addresses and domains,

24
00:02:00.715 --> 00:02:07.937
M-21-07's IPv6 milestones have all passed,
and CM-8 wants an accurate inventory.

25
00:02:07.937 --> 00:02:12.951
NIST SP 800-81 Revision
3 ties these together.

26
00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:17.913
It's guidance, not a mandate, and two of
its three authors work for a DDI vendor,

27
00:02:17.913 --> 00:02:20.930
which is worth knowing
when a vendor cites it.
